 Health and Care Excellence). Research in the UK has also focused on nutritional support and its economic implications, especially concerning malnutrition and home care.\n\nDespite its advancements, the United Kingdom faces limitations in data collection for epidemiological studies, which affects the comprehensiveness of its health data. Notably, significant epidemiological studies on intestinal rhythm have been conducted within the country. Furthermore, the UK has identified a high prevalence of dysphagia among the elderly population and estimates that hereditary fructose intolerance occurs in approximately 1 in 18,000 individuals. The country is also recognized for its clinical guidelines that describe food textures and liquid viscosities, which are essential for patient care. Overall, the United Kingdom is a key player in health research, particularly in areas related to nutrition, gastrointestinal health, and public health policy.\",17\r\n172,ALEMANIA,\"Alemania, also known as Germany, is one of the eight countries that face limitations in data collection for epidemiological studies. Despite these challenges, Germany has reported a lower prevalence of dysphagia among its population, with only 43% of the elderly affected by this condition, which is comparatively lower than in other countries.\",2\r\n171,FRANCIA,\"FRANCIA, known as France, is a country that has been highlighted in various studies regarding health issues, particularly dysphagia among older adults, indicating a significant prevalence of this condition within this demographic. Additionally, France is included in the epidemiological study of NED (Nutritional and Eating Disorders), which underscores its relevance in the context of health research. Notably, Francia was one of the pioneering countries to regulate the funding of NED in 1988, demonstrating its proactive approach to addressing nutritional and eating disorders.\",2\r\n16285,NED,\"NED is a multifaceted entity that encompasses both a nutrition